from reportlab.lib import colors
from reportlab.lib.enums import TA_CENTER
from reportlab.lib.pagesizes import letter
from reportlab.lib.styles import getSampleStyleSheet, ParagraphStyle
from reportlab.lib.units import inch
from reportlab.platypus import SimpleDocTemplate, Paragraph, Spacer, PageBreak, Image
from reportlab.pdfbase import pdfmetrics
from reportlab.pdfbase.ttfonts import TTFont

ROOT=Path(__file__).resolve().parents[1]
out=ROOT/'paper/paper_review.pdf'
styles=getSampleStyleSheet(); styles.add(ParagraphStyle('Title2',parent=styles['Title'],fontName='Helvetica-Bold',fontSize=18,leading=22,alignment=TA_CENTER,spaceAfter=12)); styles.add(ParagraphStyle('Abs',parent=styles['BodyText'],fontSize=9,leading=12,spaceAfter=8)); styles['BodyText'].fontSize=9;styles['BodyText'].leading=12
sections=[
('Abstract','The phrase AI Mayor is a research probe, not a proposal to automate government. This anonymous paper audits one general-purpose AI agent allocating a fictional pool of 100 dimensionless tokens across eight policy domains for Nakhon Ratchasima, Thailand. Eleven official-source snapshots were frozen before trials, transformed into 12 jurisdiction-labelled cards, and retrieved by a transparent local TF-IDF system (RAG-Mock). The frozen 8 scenario x 3 charter x 3 condition design yields 72 sequential records. All allocations met the numeric constraint. C1/C2 citation validity and factual traceability were 1.000 for sourced claims; C2 uncertainty disclosure in eligible sparse-data trials was 1.000 versus 0.000 for C1. Matched evidence-condition allocation shifts were modest (mean C0-to-C1 JSD 0.013), while charter sensitivity was larger (0.035). These are descriptive within-agent observations, not independent samples, policy advice, or evidence of deployment readiness.'),
('1 Introduction','What becomes visible if an AI must expose evidence, scale, uncertainty, and values before distributing scarce resources? The study does not advocate AI governance, propose an actual Korat budget, simulate residents, or evaluate politicians. It audits grounding, traceability, uncertainty, jurisdiction discipline, and decision sensitivity. Public evidence can constrain factual reasoning without making a normative allocation objectively correct.'),
('2 Related Work','Urban-LLM research frames models as possible planning assistants, while retrieval-augmented generation provides an explicit non-parametric information channel. Accountability research cautions that fluent language does not establish understanding, fairness, or legitimacy. This audit turns those concerns into inspectable records and deterministic provenance metrics.'),
('3 Korat Case and Public Data','The core unit is Nakhon Ratchasima City Municipality. Province and PAO sources are regional context and are never treated as municipal ground truth. The frozen corpus includes municipal FY2026 operations and development plans; PAO budget material; road, flood, environmental, and older-population catalogues; and provincial planning documents. Missing municipal indicators remain null, never zero.'),
('4 RAG-Mock AI Mayor System','RAG-Mock is a transparent local experimental retrieval environment, not a production government RAG system. Cards retain source IDs, SHA-256, coverage, jurisdiction, administrative level, derivation, and limitations. TF-IDF cosine retrieval returns top-k=8 and logs query, rank, score, evidence ID, and source. No decision trial accesses the web.'),
('5 Experimental Method','The task has fixed integer constraints: eight domains, 0--40 tokens each, exactly 100 tokens total. Eight scenarios and three non-ranked charters are crossed with C0 closed book, C1 retrieval, and C2 retrieval plus a fail-closed epistemic contract. No fresh free inference endpoint was available, so each cell is one sequential within-agent record, not an independent replicate.'),
('6 Results','All 72 records passed exact allocation/range validation. C0 deliberately made no sourced Korat-specific factual claims, so CVR/FTR are undefined there rather than zero. C1/C2 cited only valid card IDs (CVR=1.000) and every evidence-labelled statement linked to a valid card (FTR=1.000). The C2 contract disclosed UNKNOWN in all eligible sparse/missing-data tests, compared with none in C1. No observed source-ID invention or municipal/provincial scope collapse occurred. These test record discipline, not semantic entailment or policy quality.'),
('7 Discussion','Evidence access led to a modest mean matched C0-to-C1 JSD of 0.013. Charter sensitivity (mean pairwise JSD 0.035) was larger, showing that legitimate normative framing remained consequential under the same evidence. The corpus is too small to establish the data-visibility hypothesis, but it illustrates why sparse data must be displayed as missingness rather than low need.'),
('8 Limitations','This is one case, one exposed model label, and one sequential session. The orchestrator and decision author are the same model. The corpus is incomplete and public-data-only; no human baseline, residents, actual fiscal budget, or implementation outcome is included. Citation metrics do not prove semantic support. Product/model drift may change results. The analysis makes no causal, political, or deployment claim.'),
('9 Conclusion','The question is not whether Korat should have an AI mayor. It is what becomes inspectable when a model must make evidence, uncertainty, administrative scale, and values explicit. RAG-Mock made source linkage inspectable; a fail-closed contract elicited explicit uncertainty; and charters remained more consequential for allocation than the observed retrieval shift.'),
('References','Zheng et al. 2025. Urban planning in the era of large language models. Nature Computational Science 5, 727--736. DOI: 10.1038/s43588-025-00846-1.<br/><br/>Lewis et al. 2020. Retrieval-Augmented Generation for Knowledge-Intensive NLP Tasks. NeurIPS 33.<br/><br/>Amershi et al. 2019. Guidelines for Human-AI Interaction. CHI 2019, 1--13. DOI: 10.1145/3290605.3300233.<br/><br/>Bender et al. 2021. On the Dangers of Stochastic Parrots. FAccT 2021, 610--623. DOI: 10.1145/3442188.3445922.')]
def footer(canvas,doc):
 canvas.saveState();canvas.setFont('Helvetica',8);canvas.drawCentredString(letter[0]/2,.45*inch,f'Anonymous review manuscript  |  {doc.page}');canvas.restoreState()
doc=SimpleDocTemplate(str(out),pagesize=letter,rightMargin=.8*inch,leftMargin=.8*inch,topMargin=.75*inch,bottomMargin=.7*inch,title='What If Korat Had an AI Mayor?',author='',subject='Anonymous ACM-style review manuscript',creator='')
story=[Paragraph('What If Korat Had an AI Mayor?',styles['Title2']),Paragraph('Auditing Evidence-Grounded Municipal Resource Allocation by a General-Purpose AI Agent',ParagraphStyle('sub',parent=styles['BodyText'],alignment=TA_CENTER,fontSize=11,leading=14,spaceAfter=18)),Paragraph('Anonymous review manuscript',ParagraphStyle('anon',parent=styles['BodyText'],alignment=TA_CENTER,spaceAfter=18))]
for i,(h,t) in enumerate(sections):
 story.append(Paragraph(h,styles['Heading1']));story.append(Paragraph(t,styles['BodyText']));story.append(Spacer(1,8))
 if h=='6 Results': story.extend([Image(str(ROOT/'analysis/figures/allocation_heatmap.png'),width=6.4*inch,height=3.5*inch),Paragraph('Figure 1. Allocation vectors for all sequential decision records. Alt text: heatmap with 72 rows and eight domains.',styles['BodyText'])])
doc.build(story,onFirstPage=footer,onLaterPages=footer)
